WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.657
Bounds safety becomes harder when code computes a start position and a length separately.

2
00:00:04.657 --> 00:00:09.313
A start index can be valid by itself while the requested range is not.

3
00:00:09.313 --> 00:00:15.966
For example, a position may point to an existing element, but the requested window may extend beyond the available data.

4
00:00:15.966 --> 00:00:18.627
Validate start and length as one range decision.

5
00:00:18.627 --> 00:00:24.946
The code should check the start, the length, the calculated end, and the remaining capacity or available input together.

6
00:00:24.946 --> 00:00:31.266
Validating only one part of the pair creates a false sense of safety and leaves the combined range unresolved.

7
00:00:31.266 --> 00:00:33.594
Offset plus length calculations need arithmetic care.

8
00:00:33.594 --> 00:00:39.249
The addition can exceed the representable range, or the result can be larger than the available data.

9
00:00:39.249 --> 00:00:47.232
Safer validation often checks remaining space first, such as whether the requested length is less than or equal to total size minus start, after

10
00:00:47.232 --> 00:00:49.560
confirming that start itself is in range.

11
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.888
Subrange contracts should be explicit and testable.

12
00:00:51.888 --> 00:01:00.204
A function that accepts an offset and count should define the unit, valid bounds, behavior for empty ranges, behavior for end-of-range positions, and

13
00:01:00.204 --> 00:01:02.865
what happens when the requested range is invalid.

14
00:01:02.865 --> 00:01:06.523
That contract should be reflected in tests, not only in comments.

15
00:01:06.523 --> 00:01:08.519
Prefer bounded view abstractions when possible.

16
00:01:08.519 --> 00:01:16.835
A span, string view, iterator pair with clear ownership rules, or domain-specific range object can represent a slice more clearly than a raw pointer

17
00:01:16.835 --> 00:01:17.500
and count.

18
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.157
The view should be created only after validating that the requested window is valid.

19
00:01:22.157 --> 00:01:24.817
Boundary tests are especially important for slicing code.

20
00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:33.133
Test empty ranges, start at zero, start at the final valid position, zero-length slices, full-length slices, near-end slices, and values just beyond

21
00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:33.798
each boundary.

22
00:01:33.798 --> 00:01:39.120
These cases reveal whether the validation is checking the whole range or only pieces of it.
